# Interview Questions & Answers

## Q1. What are the first things you do during a production incident?

> First, assess customer impact and determine severity. Then establish the incident response structure and coordinate the appropriate responders. The immediate priority is to mitigate the impact and restore service, while keeping stakeholders informed.

---

## Q2. What if you don't know the root cause?

> I would not wait for the root cause before attempting mitigation. I would look for the safest effective action that can reduce customer impact and restore service, while continuing the investigation in parallel.

---

## Q3. What is the difference between mitigation and resolution?

> Mitigation reduces or eliminates the immediate customer impact. Resolution means the service has been restored and the incident is no longer actively affecting customers. Root-cause investigation and permanent corrective actions can continue afterwards.

---

## Q4. What is the difference between Incident Commander and Communications Lead?

> The Incident Commander owns and coordinates the overall incident response, while the Communications Lead manages stakeholder communication and ensures accurate, timely updates are delivered.

---

## Q5. What is a blameless postmortem?

> A blameless postmortem focuses on understanding the technical and organizational factors that allowed an incident to happen rather than blaming an individual. The goal is to identify actionable improvements that prevent recurrence.

---

## Q6. Why is communication important during incidents?

> Communication ensures that stakeholders understand the impact, current mitigation efforts and confirmed information. It prevents confusion and avoids engineers being repeatedly interrupted for status updates.

---

## Q7. Should you provide an ETA if you don't know when service will recover?

> No. I would provide the current impact, actions completed, what is currently being investigated or mitigated, and commit to the next meaningful update rather than provide an unreliable ETA.

---

## Q8. How do you prevent repeated incidents from becoming toil?

> Identify recurring manual recovery tasks and automate them where appropriate, while also investigating the underlying cause. The goal is to eliminate the recurring failure and reduce or eliminate the manual operational work.

---

